jor depressive disorder  the other peak of the sex difference  emerges in the 40s as shown on this  graph and what you can see is that this  is a time in a woman's life that  corresponds with the perimenopause which  we'll be talking about later  it's also notable that this kind of gold  standard representative study has not  been undertaken until now so there is a  similar study by kessler and gibbons  underway right now which is important  because the socio-demographic  characteristics of our country have  changed dramatically and we need updated  epidemiological data on sex differences  and mental health disorders  now the emergence of the sex difference  that puberty really  focuses us on hormones so we look at  hormonal changes at different periods of  a woman's life  so that at adolescence and puberty the  female increase in ovarian sex steroid  hormones particularly estradiol and  progesterone as well as their  metabolites which become what we call  neuroactive steroids influence brain  circuitry  we see this as well across the menstrual  cycle in studies of premenstrual  dysphoric disorder we see it in the  perinatal or around pregnancy period  and in postpartum depression we also  have a newfound appreciation for the  perimenopause is a window of hormonal  vulnerability to depressive symptoms  anxiety symptoms and psychotic disorders  and these hormone levels change in aging  of course it's not all hormones and it's  not all depression  the lifetime risk of post-traumatic  stress disorder also shows a sex  difference it's two to three times  higher in women than in men  and in part this is due to environmental  exposures so women are exposed to more  high impact trauma such as sexual trauma  compared to men and this occurs at  earlier ages  in work supported by the nih we know  that early life trauma affects the  development of brain circuits underlying  emotional regulation and mood  the lifetime prevalence of generalized  anxiety disorder which is the tendency  to worry  also shows a sex difference  and this is evident across the lifespan  but note here that this is particularly  high in magnitude at the time of the  perimenopause when women in men are  between the ages  are older than age 44.  now  women compared to men have lower rates  of substance use disorder  so these are  data from the substance abuse and mental  health service administration or samhsa  showing males and females substance use  disorders within the last 12 months  and you can see that  the one exception to this women having  lower rates of substance use disorder is  evident at the youngest ages between  ages 12 to 17.  and indeed 2019 trends compared to 2018  trends showed that substance use  disorder is increasing among those aged  12 to 17 years  particularly for females  now why is this important when we take a  lifespan in neurodevelopmental  perspective we know that exposure to  substances illicit substances  early in life during adolescence changes  brain circuits involved in reward and  involved in the pleasure taking of  substances making it very difficult much  more difficult for people to overcome  substance use disorders  when we look at epidemiological change  trends the use of ellipse illicit  substances and marijuana in those 12  years and older increased over the  lifetime for females but not for males  although use in the past year and past  month increased for both sexes  now substance use disorders are more  common in men than women but there are  very important sex differences in  substance use disorders and work funded  by  nida has  demonstrated myriad differences these  include women having higher rates of  co-occurring psychiatric disorders  women are more likely to have a  co-occurring mood disorder 7.5 times  greater or an anxiety disorder 4.2 times  greater nevertheless they're less likely  to receive treatment and they face  particular barriers to treatment  including some of the stigma surrounding  treatment of substance use disorders of  pregnancy  we know that stress induces craving  and work by nida shows differences in  stress-induced dopamine transmission and  women show greater  cravings with stress compared to men  and importantly in women again with a  lifespan perspective the course of  substance use disorder in women is more  strongly linked to childhood trauma  now in women there is a circle  of mental health intersecting with  trauma that sustains and perpetuates  substance use disorders  so that trauma particularly early life  trauma and its related mental health  problems lead to self-medication  of females  and that in turn leads to addiction  which makes women more vulnerable to  victimization which in turn influences  um  or increases their exposure to  additional trauma and mental health  problems so this is a vicious circle for  women and one that  illustrates the importance of the  intersection between  substance use disorders and mental  health disorders with childhood  exposures and continued trauma in women  so let's talk a bit about childhood  work in adverse childhood experiences  shows some sex differences  in that women experience a greater  number of frequent  aces  so women showed more they were more  likely to have more than four aces and  in work done  increasingly across the united states  funded by the national institutes of  health including beautiful work by neil  epperson  shows that these aces have  especially early in life have long  standing influences on brain circuits  and vulnerability to mood disorders  now we think about adverse childhood  events as influencing mental health and  substance use disorder which they do but  it's quite astounding when we look at  the proportion of these disorders that's  explained by these aces so for example  24 of the individual differences  in heavy drinking  are attributable to aces and for  depression 44  of the variance is explained by aces in  other words these adverse childhood  events account for  much of the risk of these disorders  and it's not just mental health  disorders when we look at coronary heart  disease or chd here we see that adverse  childhood events influence  cardiovascular health and this shows the  importance of work by the nhlbi  in mental health as well  childhood sexual abuse is a particularly  important traumatic event to consider in  a lifespan perspective on women's mental  health and there is a very big  difference in  the frequency of sexual abuse between  girls and boys indeed one in four women  and  compared to one in 13 men report having  been sexually abused as a child a  considerable diff  difference  and in women women with a history of  childhood sexual abuse are at in  increased risk of developing  a variety of different mental health  disorders and  they're twice as likely for example  to develop  anxiety disorders major depression  alcohol use disorders drug  dependents and the like and they're also  likely to have more comorbidities more  than one psychiatric disorder so that's  true for any childhood sexual abuse but  exposure to intercourse at during  childhood is predicted particularly  toxic with these odds ratios over five  so that women with this exposure early  in life have a five-fold increased risk  of drug dependence and of having more  than one  disorder so this is an important example  of how we need to focus on childhood  trauma and sexual abuse from a public  health perspective if we want to  optimize women's mental health across  the lifespan  let's talk about puberty now  so we know that the sex difference in  major depressive disorder begins at  puberty and there's work that also shows  sex differences in the trajectories of  depressive symptoms  so for example in girls the age at peak  velocity velocity of depressive symptoms  is younger than that of boys it's  earlier so it starts 13.5 years and  girls compared to 16.4 years in boys  this kind of research informed screening  efforts to suggest that women girls need  to be screened earlier than boys  now work done by monique ernst in the  intramural research program of the  national institutes of health shows that  puberty is accompanied by  measurable changes in brain circuits  that aren't explained by age alone  and these brain circuits underlie  emotional regulation in brain systems  that are implicated in depression and  anxiety  so when we look at these different brain  circuits what we can see at puberty is  that girls brain connectivity that is  the the extent to which one region of  the brain  is  speaking if you will to another area of  the brain that decreases in brain  systems that underlie emotional  regulation whereas boys show an  increased connectivity in those brain  circuits  so how do we know that this is an  important neurobiological difference  well lower connectivity in these brain  circuits in girls was associated with  more severe internalizing problems later  in adolescence so these brain changes  were predictive of symptoms of  depression and anxiety  later in adolescence  and so this kind of work funded by the  nih illustrates how puberty might  amplify the risk for internalizing  symptoms in girls leading again with a  lifespan perspective to an increased  vulnerability to depression and anxiety  okay let's go now to college  we know that nationally there is an  increase in the prevalence of anxiety  depressive disorders and panic attacks  among american college students  and indeed the prevalence uh is  astounding particularly for females with  almost 28  of females uh having an anxiety disorder  and 22 percent of females having a  depressive disorder and panic attacks at  14.8 percent compared to much lower  rates half of that  in males again showing college is an  important inflection point in women's  mental health  we are in the middle of a global  pandemic  students are working from home and it's  interesting that a  new survey research from the healthy  mind students and a very large sample of  individuals from 14 campuses  shows an interesting change  in substance use disorders excuse me  there is an increase in depressive  disorders  but a decrease in suicidal ideation and  a decrease in illicit substance use in  binge drinking however the depressive  symptoms that are increasing during this  pandemic are also associated with  impairment in academic performance again  showing the importance of mental health  disorders to daily functioning in this  case in college students  in the last few years there's been a lot  of appropriate attention to sexual  assault in women with a metoo movement  and the prevalence of  sexual assault in college-age students  is considerably higher in females  compared to males  now  mental health in college is a general  issue but it's important to recognize  that sexual minorities show a higher  prevalence of these disorders and indeed  show a four to five-fold increased risk  in self-harm suicidal thoughts and  suicidal attempts so the most severe  forms of mental health are higher in  sexual minorities  particularly women so this underscores  the importance of targeted efforts at  prevention  let's talk now about pregnancy  when a lot of people think about women's  mental health they think about  postpartum depression there are a lot of  lessons that we've learned by studying  pregnancy i think that can inform our  understanding at other reproductive  milestones in a woman's life  what's particularly important i think is  to consider the perinatal period as a  window of opportunity for the  identification and treatment of a  variety of different mental health  disorders  even if those are not new disorders in  other words even if they did not arise  de novo during pregnancy  women of color and rural women in  particular  have limited access to mental health  services and by implementing mental  health screenings in the perinatal  period we can oftentimes for the first  time begin to screen and identify these  disorders in these disadvantaged  populations  cdc data show that the rates of pregnant  women with a depression diagnosis and  delivery increased by sevenfold  from 2000 to 2015  and subsequent data showed that this  trend actually continues  so  women in the perinatal period show a  very high prevalence of just of  depression  now this is particularly important to  consider for women of color because  compared to white women the odds of  depression prior to delivery were  fivefold higher in black women twofold  higher in latinas and this disparity  persists in the postpartum period the  disparities also continue when we  examine uh delivery of mental health  care now delivery  of mental health care is poor all around  but it's even worse for women of color  why is this important maternal mortality  in the united states is increasing  while it's decreasing in other countries  and we think about uh depression in  particular suicidality is being an  important contributor to maternal  morbidity  indeed a very recent study  in jama psychiatry showed a  considerable increase in suicidal  ideation  and in self-harm  over the last decade this was  particularly true for young women and  women of color as well as women with  psychiatric comorbidities including  anxiety depression and comorbid  psychosis so  maternal mortality in the united states  is an important consideration in women's  mental health  small efforts efforts at simply  screening women during the perinatal  period pay off so in absolute terms just  the implementation of mental health  screenings in the perinatal period  reduces depression prevalence by two to  nine percent and in terms of women with  a psychiatric diagnosis the screening  can reduce remission 10 to 34  in absolute terms so the importance of  screening can't be underestimated  now  science has advanced our understanding  of how to better detect  psychiatric diagnoses in women so one  approach is computerized adaptive  diagnostics  here i highlight the work of robert  gibbons a colleague from the university  of chicago who is implementing a new  approach to the the identification of  mental health disorders essentially this  is a technology based approach that  draws items in a screener  from a very deep item bank  and tailors the screening to each woman  depending upon her response to the  previous question  we have implemented this work in our own  perinatal research study and i'd like to  show you some initial data to illustrate  to you the importance of screening as  well as the importance of screening for  more than one psychiatric diagnosis in  the perinatal period  so in our clinic which serves women of  color living in chicago  we find that  21  of women actually screen positive for  major depressive disorder at some point  during pregnancy or their very early  postpartum period  so among the women with depression um  you can see that the majority had only  major depressive disorder but look at  the percentage of women who had a  comorbid diagnosis six percent of women  for example had  generalized anxiety disorder post  traumatic stress disorder and substance  use disorder again illustrating  comorbidities as a very important  consideration and also illustrating the  value of techniques like computerized  adaptive diagnostics for efficiently  quickly and sensitively detecting these  different  mental health disorders in the clinic  setting  there are also novel approaches to  treatment delivery here i highlight the  work of mike o'hara and jenna duffy with  their sunny side an evidence-based  cognitive behavior therapy approach that  can be delivered on the internet free of  charged without stigma of a clinic visit  without needing to get a babysitter and  it's been shown to prevent perinatal  depression  in the population so technological  advances in screening and in the  treatment of  psychiatric diagnose diagnoses are very  important and evidence shows many of  these studies supported by the nih that  these online treatments are as effective  as brief face-to-face  treatments and of course it's not enough  to use cognitive behavior therapy some  women need pharmacological treatment and  one of the great successes over the past  decade was the development of roxanne  alone an aloe pregnant  aloe pregnant alone based treatment that  is a metabolite of progesterone  for postpartum depression and this has  been shown to very quickly and  effectively treat postpartum depression  and it's now available  in pill form which is also shown great  of effectiveness so what this shows us  is that understanding the  neurobiological underpinnings that is  the hormonal neuroactive steroid  underpinnings of mental health disorders  at critical times like the perinatal  period can inform novel treatments for  mental health disorders  it's important to treat mental health  disorders in pregnancy not just for mom  but also for her offspring this is  neuro-imaging work functional magnetic  resonance work that's been done in  six-month-old children of moms either  with or without perinatal depression and  what they showed is that six months of  age in these offspring you could detect  differences in the emotional regulation  circuitry showing the importance again  of treating  antenatal mood disorders in women to  benefit the offspring  let's move now to adulthood  there's been a lot of talk about  harassment and discrimination in the  workplace  and this is very common  but one thing i'd like to point out is  the intersectionality of this experience  so that women of mixed race or ethnicity  experience the highest levels of this  exposure overall  in this exposure harassment and  discrimination in turn leads to  increases in problem drinking and mental  health disorders  particularly among women  work by my good colleague rebecca  thurston at the university of pittsburgh  has shown the importance of these  exposures not to mental health  specifically but rather to physical  health  so that there was a twofold higher odds  of women having hypertension if they had  high levels of harassment in their daily  life  one of the signature programs of the  office of research on women's health is  the building interdisciplinary research  careers in women's health a birch k-12  program which funds  young investigators to pursue  studies of women's health or sex and  gender-based  medicine in health  our birch colleagues uh and my co-pi of  the uic bridge irene irena bahimshi  completed a survey of over  500 faculty at our own institution to  look at work life and stress before and  after covid and what you can see here  is that there's high levels of stress  but women carrying more of it it's  impacted their scholarly productivity  their teaching their advising their  committee responsibilities  and their own personal health  so work stress  is an important consideration menopause  is also important now the perimenopause  is a window of vulnerability for  depression  and for  subclinical depressive symptoms  and we can see here in work by joyce  bromberger and the study of women's  health across the nation a doubling at  any point in time of the prevalence of  peri of depression in the perimenopause  and post-menopause compared to the  pre-menopause  most women who experience depression at  this time in life are women with a  history of depression prior to the  menopause and so this represents a  recurrence of their depressive disorder  but it's not just depression  an excellent article published in the  popular press by lisa miller  it published in new york magazine  highlighted the importance of psychotic  disorders in women at the time of the  menopause and epidemiological studies  show a second increase in the  prevalence of these disorders or the  risk of these disorders at the menopause  so we need to take our lens beyond just  depression and look at other psychiatric  diagnoses  hormonal contributions are important  there's proof of concept for example  that the use of estrogen can dampen  psychotic symptoms in women at this time  but it's not just the hormonal changes  at menopause it's a variety of things  it's biological factors health related  factors and psychosocial factors that  affect a woman's mental health and then  we have the later years in a woman's  life  this is a gender difference caregiving  typically falls to women two-thirds of  caregivers are women  particularly in the field of dementia so  wives take care of their husbands with  dementia and daughters do the same and  when women are caregivers they spend  more time caregiving than men  and these women who provide this  caregiving request more mental health  services  and they are not widely available  the irony is that depression is  associated with a 36 increased risk of  cognitive decline in women so women  serve as caregivers for their husbands  in  with alzheimer's disease and  nevertheless the mental health sequelae  of that experience contributes to their  own risk for cognitive disorders  so it's important to recognize this  intersection between mental health and  cognitive health  so today we've done a survey of women's  mental health across the lifespan we've  talked about childhood puberty college  pregnancy  adulthood menopause in the later years  taking a lifespan  perspective and i hope this has  illustrated the importance and the  success of the work  that's being done at the nih in this  important question and how it intersects  with other nih institutes including the  national institute on  drug abuse and  the nih lbi  i'm indebted to my very good colleagues  for their collaboration in my work over  the last 30 years  and i also want to  personally congratulate dr clayton  and dr vivian pinn on the 30th  anniversary of the office of research on  women's health in a special thing to  reach thanks to regine de tao for  inviting me to come here it's been a  great honor thank you

